# Consolidate every origin onto promote-species.app. The kill-switch
# rules come first: a service worker on a retired origin can never
# update through a redirect (its sw.js fetch follows the 301 and fails),
# so the old script url serves public/sw-kill.js, which wipes the caches
# and unregisters itself; the next navigation then follows the 301 to
# the canonical origin. The domain-scoped rules only match these exact
# hosts — promote-species.app, Netlify deploy previews and localhost
# fall through to the rules below unchanged. 301s keep path and query
# (:splat). NB: redirects configured in the Netlify UI (domain
# management) take precedence over this file — the www aliases currently
# 301 to arten-fördern.app there and should be retargeted to
# promote-species.app (or removed, so these rules fire directly).
https://xn--arten-frdern-bjb.app/sw.js  /sw-kill.js  200!
https://xn--arten-frdern-bjb.ch/sw.js   /sw-kill.js  200!
https://arten-foerdern.app/sw.js        /sw-kill.js  200!
https://arten-foerdern.ch/sw.js         /sw-kill.js  200!
https://xn--arten-frdern-bjb.app/*      https://promote-species.app/:splat 301!
https://xn--arten-frdern-bjb.ch/*       https://promote-species.app/:splat 301!
https://arten-foerdern.app/*            https://promote-species.app/:splat 301!
https://arten-foerdern.ch/*             https://promote-species.app/:splat 301!
https://www.xn--arten-frdern-bjb.app/*  https://promote-species.app/:splat 301!
https://www.xn--arten-frdern-bjb.ch/*   https://promote-species.app/:splat 301!
https://www.arten-foerdern.app/*        https://promote-species.app/:splat 301!
https://www.arten-foerdern.ch/*         https://promote-species.app/:splat 301!
https://www.promote-species.app/*       https://promote-species.app/:splat 301!

# Proxy backend endpoints same-origin for arten-fördern.ch (Netlify):
# auth needs same-origin because session cookies can never cross the
# .ch/.app TLD boundary (SameSite); PostgREST's CORS only allows .app.
# The .app frontend keeps calling the API subdomains directly.
#
# The SPA's login page lives at EXACTLY /auth — without the explicit rule
# below, the /auth/* proxy also matches the bare path (the splat matches
# empty) and Netlify forwards it to the auth server, which 404s
# ("Cannot GET /auth/"). Everything else under /auth/* (API calls, the
# OAuth callback) belongs to the auth server.
# NB: a rule for /auth/ (trailing slash) is futile — netlify matches it to
# the /auth/* proxy with an empty splat no matter what comes first; nothing
# generates /auth/ links, so it stays a 404.
/auth     /index.html  200
/auth/*  https://auth.xn--arten-frdern-bjb.app/auth/:splat  200
/api/*   https://api.xn--arten-frdern-bjb.app/:splat        200

/*    /index.html   200
